WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.962
Consistency asks whether the same concept is represented the same way.

00:00:03.962 --> 00:00:11.127
Mixed date formats, category labels, and units can create serious interpretation problems.

00:00:11.127 --> 00:00:14.317
Timeliness is different. It asks whether the data is current enough for the decision.

00:00:14.317 --> 00:00:21.602
A fifteen-minute delay may be excellent for a monthly reporting process but unacceptable for an emergency

00:00:21.602 --> 00:00:23.432
monitoring system.

00:00:23.432 --> 00:00:28.517
Both dimensions require documentation: shared definitions for consistency and timestamps or refresh

00:00:28.517 --> 00:00:34.602
expectations for timeliness. In practice, quality rules should be tied to a use case.

00:00:34.602 --> 00:00:39.977
A fifteen-minute delay may be acceptable for a monthly report but unacceptable for a real-time alert.

00:00:39.977 --> 00:00:46.597
Likewise, an unusual physiological value should often be flagged and investigated rather than automatically

00:00:46.597 --> 00:00:51.704
deleted. Good cleaning preserves evidence and documents uncertainty.
